# Fedory core desktop package list, installed by install/config/base-packages.sh.
#
# Names here are upstream Omarchy package names, resolved to their Fedora
# dnf/COPR/Flatpak equivalent by fedory-pkg-add via packaging/package-map.tsv
# (same mechanism every other install/*.sh leaf uses) -- this is the direct
# Fedora-native equivalent of upstream's install/omarchy-base.packages,
# pacstrapped by the Arch ISO. Fedory has no ISO to pacstrap from (see
# docs/scope.md), so this file only exists to be read by
# install/config/base-packages.sh at bootstrap/setup time.
#
# This list is everything a Fedory desktop always gets. Software a user can
# decline at install time lives in install/groups/*.packages instead -- an
# office suite and video tools (office-media), and printing/mDNS (printing).
# bootstrap.sh offers those as opt-out choices; see install/helpers/groups.sh.
# Declining a group is skip-on-install only and never uninstalls anything.
#
# Package names NOT listed here, from upstream's own omarchy-base.packages,
# and why:
#   - docker, docker-buildx, docker-compose: external-repo status (Docker's
#     own dnf repo, not COPR) -- installed by install/config/base-packages.sh
#     directly, not via this list.
#   - mise: source-build status (official install script) -- installed by
#     install/config/base-packages.sh directly, not via this list.
#   - aether, cliamp, omacut, omawrite, omarchy-nvim, tensaku, tobi-try:
#     Basecamp first-party AUR-only tools with no confirmed independent
#     source repo (see packaging/package-map.tsv) -- not installable yet.
#   - dua-cli, lazydocker, lazygit, python-terminaltexteffects, starship,
#     tree-sitter-cli, tzupdate, usage: installed per-user through mise wrappers by
#     install/user/source-tools.sh.
#   - asdcontrol, lua51, ttf-ia-writer: hardware-specific, compatibility-only,
#     or manually licensed assets that still need dedicated setup.
#   - bluez-utils, expac, kernel-modules-hook, pacman-contrib, ufw,
#     ufw-docker, yay: dropped status -- Arch-specific tooling with no
#     Fedora equivalent needed (see packaging/package-map.tsv for why each
#     one specifically).

